            <ArticleTitle>Predictive factors for extraction site hernia after laparoscopic right colectomy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1323-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00384-016-2610-x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Incisional hernia at the extraction site (ESIH) is a common complication after laparoscopic colorectal resections. The aim of this study was to evaluate the prevalence and potential risk factors for ESIH in a large cohort study having standardized technique.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A cross-sectional study was performed including all patients who underwent elective laparoscopic right or extended right colectomy for cancer from November 2006 to October 2013 using a standard technique. All patients have been followed up for a minimum of 1 year with abdominal CT scan.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 292 patients were included with a median follow-up of 42 months. Twenty patients (6.8 %) developed ESIH. Obesity (odds ratio (OR) = 3.76, 95 % confidence interval (CI) 1.39-10.15; p = 0.009) and incision length (OR 2.86, 95 % CI 1.077-7.60; p = 0.035) significantly predisposed to the development of ESIH.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This study identified that the risk of ESIH is significant after colonic resections and there are several risk factors responsible for the development of ESIH.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
